From f6cc5d41bbb4a729fdcb30d6dc08d883dcfe900a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Campos Ramos <Capitain_Jack@yahoo.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 02:50:57 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] greeter: Draw the wallpaper below the interface where it
 shows depth

The lock screen's wallpaper is a wallpaper: on a stereo screen that
shows depth it lies behind the screen as the desktop's does, while the
clock, the password field and the rest of the interface stay at the
screen. Stereo KWin can only place it there when the wallpaper is a
surface of its own; the greeter drew it into the interface's window,
under the interface (z -1000).

On Wayland each screen's view gets a child QQuickWindow for the
wallpaper: frameless, transparent for input, with an alpha channel (a
video wallpaper's stereo layer lies below it), black, the view's size,
and Qt::WindowStaysOnBottomHint, which the edition's Qt Wayland turns
into a subsurface below the view's surface and confirms on the window
(_q_wayland_below_parent). A Qt that does not confirm it would put the
window above the interface and hide the password field, so there the
wallpaper stays in the interface's window and the greeter warns once.
updateWallpaperLayer() moves
the wallpaper there, makes the view transparent and offers the window's
content item as wallpaperLayer, for the lock screen's fader, which
draws a copy of the wallpaper (its blur samples the wallpaper item; an
item belongs to one window at a time, so the wallpaper leaves its window
before the fader moves and joins the fader's window after it), while:
- the compositor says the view is awake (kde_stereo_depth_v1, through
  libstereo-declare 1.5): it is on a stereo screen;
- the user's Background depth is above zero (kwinrc [StereoDepth]
  SunkLimit, 10 by default as in KWin; read once, since it cannot
  change while the screen is locked and the greeter starts at each
  lock);
- the stereo show is not on.
Otherwise the wallpaper stays in the interface's window, drawn exactly
as before: on a 2D screen and with a Background depth of 0 nothing
changes (an interface drawn over a transparent window loses its
subpixel text and blends its edges in the compositor, so the layer is
only used where the wallpaper does move). On X11 nothing changes.

Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
